--TEST--
A property access through a plain `object` receiver is keyed on the written name at run time, reads and writes, and throws on a name the concrete class does not carry — `rule:types/erased-member-access`
--FILE--
<?nvs
// covers: lang:classes/object-the-top-of-every-class-type
// `rule:types/erased-member-access`'s fully *erased* half. A receiver typed `object` carries no
// field list at all, so the access answers `mixed` and nothing about it is
// known until the concrete class is in hand: the fetch is keyed on the
// written name, the write is checked against what that class declares the
// field to hold, and a name it does not carry is a catchable throw rather
// than a silent null.
class Erased {
    public int $count = 0;
    public string $label = "left";

    public function constructor() { }

    // The receiver's own class is whatever the caller passed — a named class
    // here, a shape below, and this body cannot tell the two apart.
    public static function show(object $o): void {
        echo $o->label, "\n";
    }

    public static function relabel(object $o): void {
        $o->label = "right";
    }

    // The write's value is checked against the *concrete* field's declared
    // type, which is the one thing an erased write site cannot know for
    // itself.
    public static function refuse(object $o): string {
        try {
            $o->count = "not a number";
            return "not refused";
        } catch (Throwable $e) {
            return $e->message;
        }
    }

    public static function missingRead(object $o): string {
        try {
            echo $o->nope, "\n";
            return "not refused";
        } catch (Throwable $e) {
            return $e->message;
        }
    }

    public static function missingWrite(object $o): string {
        try {
            $o->nope = 1;
            return "not refused";
        } catch (Throwable $e) {
            return $e->message;
        }
    }
}

// A shape value behind an `object` binding: both fields are reachable by
// name, and neither is at a slot this file could have named.
var $point = {x: 1, label: "shape"};
object $erased = $point;
echo $erased->x, " ", $erased->label, "\n";

// The write lands on the value itself, not on a copy — a shape is an
// ordinary refcounted instance (`rule:types/anonymous-object`) and `object` erases only the
// static view of it.
$erased->label = "wrote";
echo $point->label, "\n";

var $obj = new Erased();
Erased::show($obj);
Erased::relabel($obj);
echo $obj->label, " ", $obj->count, "\n";

echo Erased::refuse($obj), "\n";
echo Erased::missingRead($obj), "\n";
// A shape's synthesized class is named for its field *names*, sorted.
echo Erased::missingWrite($point), "\n";
--EXPECT--
1 shape
wrote
left
right 0
`Erased` declares field `count` as int, so a string cannot be written to it
`Erased` has no field `nope`
`$shape{label,x}` has no field `nope`
